FADE IN:

EXT. ROCKY COASTLINE - DAWN

Waves hammer black rocks. On the headland, a white LIGHTHOUSE stands against a bruised sky, its lamp dark.

A rusted pickup rattles up the gravel track and stops.

MARA OKAFOR (40s, weathered, practical) climbs out with a canvas tool bag. She stares up at the tower.

MARA
Forty years it never missed a night.

THEO (O.S.)
It missed last night.

THEO BRANDT (60s, wiry, wool cap) steps out from behind the keeper's cottage, holding a dead flashlight.

THEO
Ships came close. Too close.

MARA
(beat)
Then let's not make it two.

INT. LIGHTHOUSE - STAIRWELL - CONTINUOUS

A spiral of iron steps winds upward into darkness. Mara climbs, counting under her breath. Her boots ring on every step. Somewhere above, something *drips*.

THEO
(calling up)
Hundred and twelve! Don't trust the ninetieth!

Mara reaches the ninetieth step. It groans. She skips it.

INT. LIGHTHOUSE - LAMP ROOM - MOMENTS LATER

The great Fresnel lens fills the room like a glass hive. Mara sets down her bag and runs a hand along the brass housing. Her fingers come away black with soot.

MARA
Somebody's been burning something up here.

She kneels. Under the lens, wedged in the gears: a small leather NOTEBOOK, singed at the edges.

She opens it. Pages of tight handwriting. Dates. Ship names. Coordinates.

MARA (CONT'D)
Theo? Whose handwriting is this?

THEO (O.S.)
(faint, from below)
What?

MARA
I said whose--

The lamp FLARES to life. Blinding white. Mara throws up an arm.

CUT TO:

EXT. ROCKY COASTLINE - NIGHT

The beam sweeps the water. Steady. Patient.

Far out on the black water, a SHIP'S LIGHT answers: three short flashes.

INT. KEEPER'S COTTAGE - NIGHT

A cramped kitchen. A kettle shrieks. Theo pours two mugs of tea with shaking hands. Mara sits at the table, the notebook open between them.

MARA
These dates go back to before you were born.

THEO
My father kept the light. His father before him.

MARA
And they wrote down every ship that passed.

THEO
Not every ship.

He sets down the mugs. Sits. Looks at the notebook like it might bite.

THEO (CONT'D)
Only the ones that weren't supposed to be there. My father said the light doesn't just warn ships away from the rocks. Some nights, it calls them home. And some of the ships that answer have been at the bottom of the sea for a hundred years.

MARA
That's a ghost story.

THEO
That's a logbook.

Mara turns a page. The last entry is dated TONIGHT. The ink is still wet.

MARA
(whispering)
That's not possible.

THEO ^
You see it now.

She stares at him. Then at the window, where the beam sweeps past, and in the instant of light, a TALL-MASTED SHIP is anchored in the cove.

SMASH CUT TO:

EXT. COVE - CONTINUOUS

Mara runs down the path to the shingle beach, flashlight bobbing. The ship is gone. Only fog.

At the waterline, wet footprints lead OUT of the sea and up the beach, toward the lighthouse.

Mara follows them with the flashlight beam. They stop at her own feet.

MARA
(quietly)
Okay.

She looks up at the tower. The lamp turns. Turns.

FADE OUT.
